ReplacementChunks.ReplacementContent:
\item \textbf{FORC protocol implementation (V67--V69)}: introduction of the First-Order Reversal Curve (FORC) field mode (\code{forc}) with saturation/reversal field parameter control (\code{--forc\_Br}, \code{--forc\_t\_sat}, \code{--forc\_t\_rev}), real-time dual-panel video monitoring of the minor reversal loop, and automated export to \code{forc\_curve.csv}. Added \code{--forc\_sweep} choices to filter branch tracking.
\item \textbf{Sinusoidal Demagnetization (V70)}: implementation of a pre-treatment sinusoidal alternating field demagnetization (AFD) procedure (\code{--demag on}), sweeping the field from $+B_{\mathrm{ext}}$ to zero, decaying 30\% per cycle, at a configurable frequency \code{--demag\_freq}.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\textbf{(7) forc} --- First-Order Reversal Curve (FORC) suite. The system simulates a complete family of first-order reversal curves. Each cycle saturates the array at $+B_{\max}$, ramps down to a reversal field $B_{r, k}$ (spaced evenly from $+B_{\max}$ to the minimum reversal field \code{--forc\_Br\_min}), and sweeps back to $+B_{\max}$. The sweep is repeated for a total of \code{--forc\_n\_curves} cycles. Timing is automatically calculated so that the total time is $t_{\mathrm{sim}} = N_{\mathrm{curves}} \cdot (t_{\mathrm{sat}} + t_{\mathrm{ramp\_down}} + t_{\mathrm{ramp\_up}})$. The parameter \code{--forc\_sweep} (\code{both}, \code{up}, or \code{down}; V69) controls which sweep branches (decreasing, increasing, or both) are logged and plotted. The entire suite of curves is logged to \code{forc\_curve.csv} containing the reversal field $B_r$ as a fifth column:
\begin{verbatim}
# Run a family of 30 FORC curves, tracing both branches, sweeping Br down to -5 mT
python3 compass.py --N 20 --M 20 --field_mode forc \
    --ext_Bx 0.005 --forc_Br_min -0.005 --forc_n_curves 30 --forc_sweep both
\end{verbatim}

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Demagnetization procedure (V70)} --- Decaying sinusoidal alternating field demagnetization (AFD). Before any field mode is executed, an optional pre-treatment phase can be enabled (\code{--demag on}). The field oscillates along the axis defined by \code{--phi\_ext\_deg} with an envelope that starts at $+B_{\max}$ and decays by 30\% in each cycle (intensity is multiplied by $0.7$ per period) at a frequency of \code{--demag\_freq} Hz for a duration of \code{--demag\_cycles} cycles:
\begin{verbatim}
# Run a static field simulation after demagnetizing the sample for 20 cycles
python3 compass.py --N 10 --M 10 --B_ext 0.005 --demag on --t_sim 1.0
\end{verbatim}
During the demagnetization pre-treatment phase, the physical integration is carried out to prepare the initial configuration of the needles, but data logging (CSVs, figures, and video frames) starts recording exactly at $t_{\mathrm{phys}} = 0.0$ when the demagnetization completes and the primary field mode begins.

\noindent In \code{hysteresis}, the $M(B)$ loop is exported to \code{hysteresis\_loop.csv}; in \code{forc}, to \code{forc\_curve.csv}; in \code{sine}, to \code{sine\_field.csv}. In pulse/step modes, \code{--torque\_tol} controls the equilibrium criterion and \code{--t\_sim\_full 1} disables early stopping (Appendix~A).

V37 & 06/07/2026 & Documented V69 improvements (new parameter \code{--forc\_sweep} with choices \code{both}/\code{up}/\code{down}, and video dimension divisible-by-2 bug fix). \\
V38 & 06/07/2026 & Documented V70 demagnetization procedure (\code{--demag}, \code{--demag\_freq}, \code{--demag\_cycles}), updating parameter lists, capability descriptions, and usage recipes. \\
\end{longtable}

